decisions:
  - id: D-single-owner
    decision: AppDelegate is the sole lifecycle owner of all stateful components
    rationale: SwiftUI @StateObject creates ownership at the view layer, but views are ephemeral — the app delegate persists for the entire app lifecycle. Components like InputOrchestrator need to outlive any individual view presentation.
  - id: D-voiceArm-action
    decision: EyebrowStateMachine emits .voiceArm on arming transitions instead of nil
    rationale: The VoiceControlCoordinator has an explicit .armed state that the HUD visualizes (yellow). Without the action, arm() was never called and the HUD skipped from idle (gray) directly to recording (red). The action makes the state machine's output complete — every meaningful transition produces an action.
  - id: D-provider-lifecycle
    decision: Providers are created/destroyed from UserDefaults preferences, not hardcoded
    rationale: 'Users may enable/disable gaze, gestures, or voice mid-session from Settings. The orchestrator needs to handle hot-swapping: stop → reconfigure providers → restart. Creating unused providers wastes resources (camera access, Python subprocess).'
  - id: D-observed-not-owned
    decision: MainOverlayView uses @ObservedObject instead of @StateObject for orchestrator and workspace
    rationale: '@StateObject means the view creates and owns the object. @ObservedObject means something else owns it and the view just watches. Since AppDelegate owns these, the view must observe, not own. This eliminates the duplicate instances.'
learnings:
  - Tests passing does not mean the system works — integration wiring (who creates, who starts, who passes) is a separate verification concern from unit correctness
  - SwiftUI @StateObject creates ownership at the view layer, which is wrong for app-lifetime components — use @ObservedObject and pass from the true lifecycle owner
  - When a state machine transition is silent (returns nil), downstream consumers that depend on being notified will silently fail — every meaningful transition should produce an action
  - 'The pattern of ''create providers conditionally from preferences'' enables hot-swapping: stop orchestrator, reconfigure, restart — no app restart needed'
